<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN>

<PLUGIN
  name="fanctrlplus2"
  author="ck9393 / andrebrait"
  version="1.9.4"
  launch="Settings/fanctrlplus2"
  pluginURL="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/main/plugin/fanctrlplus2.plg"
  min="6.9.0"
  support="https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/issues"
  project="https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus"
  readme="https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus#readme"
>

<ICON>
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/images/fanctrlplus2.png
</ICON>

<CHANGES>
<![CDATA[

### 1.9.4

## What's changed

- **Number fields keep what you type** (#17). The Interval field has a 5-second minimum, and typing `20` gave `50`: the `2` was raised to the minimum before the `0` arrived. Now the minimum applies when you leave the field, and the maximum still applies as you type. A value below the minimum gets an orange outline and a tooltip naming it. The same applies to the percentage and temperature fields. (#18)
- Adding a fan or a disk group no longer resets a field you are typing in. (#18)

## Upgrade compatibility

No configuration changes. The server still clamps the interval to 5–3600 seconds on save. Reload the settings page after upgrading so the browser picks up the new script.

## Verification

All 28 test files pass. The new test fails on 1.9.3. Typing into the real input was checked in headless Chromium. It has not yet been checked on a real Unraid server.

### 1.9.3

## What's changed

- **Dashboard widget: one value per column** (#13). The temperature cell packed the value and its source into fixed-width slots, so "40 °C" ran into the source, and a long disk-group name was cut mid-word and drawn over the RPM column on hover. The widget now has one value per column, with units in the headers: Name, Temp (°C), Source, Speed (RPM), PWM (%), Status and Run. (#16)
 - Values never spill into the next column. Long names and sources end in "…" and show in full on hover.
 - Numbers are right-aligned, and Status and Run are narrower.
 - On narrow screens the headers are abbreviated (°C, RPM, %) and each fan's source moves to a second line under the fan.
 - An idling fan's temperature shows `--` instead of `*`; the Source column says "Idle".
- Fan names are now HTML-escaped in the widget.

## Upgrade compatibility

No configuration changes. Reload the dashboard after upgrading so the browser picks up the new layout.

## Verification

All 28 test files pass. The widget was rendered from the real page output with Unraid's dashboard stylesheet in headless Chromium at tile widths from 340 to 780 pixels ([renders](https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/pull/16#issuecomment-6014405931)). It has not yet been checked on a real Unraid dashboard.

### 1.9.2

## What's changed

- **Warning for MSI boards whose system fans ignore speed changes** (#13). On some MSI boards the nct6687 driver accepts speed changes for the system fans (`pwm3` and up) but does not apply them, so those fans stay at about 60%. The driver fixes this with its `msi_fan_brute_force` module option. The settings page now shows the workaround when a configured fan is affected and the option is off. The option's state is read from the driver itself where possible: its `fan_control_watchdog` attribute or its "MSI fan brute force mode" kernel log line, falling back to `/etc/modprobe.d`. (#15)
- **Notification when such a fan is stuck** (#15). While an affected fan is set above 60% but reads back 60% or less for 10 seconds, the plugin sends one Unraid warning notification per fan per boot. The check runs on the fan's control interval, so with longer intervals the warning comes on the second tick.

## Workaround

On Unraid, run these from a terminal, then reboot:

```
mkdir -p /boot/config/modprobe.d
echo "options nct6687 msi_fan_brute_force=1" >> /boot/config/modprobe.d/nct6687.conf
```

If `modinfo nct6687 | grep msi_fan_brute_force` prints nothing, the installed driver predates the option and needs an update from the Nuvoton NCT6687 Driver plugin. See the [driver documentation](https://github.com/Fred78290/nct6687d#loading-msi_fan_brute_force-parameter-fails).

## Upgrade compatibility

No configuration changes. Boards that do not use the nct6687 driver's MSI layout are unaffected.

## Verification

All 28 test files pass. The fan loop was run against a simulated nct6687 controller: a fan pinned at 60% produced one notification on the second 10-second tick, and a fan that followed its target produced none. The reporter of #13 confirmed the workaround fixes their fans. The detection has not yet been run on real affected hardware.


For older releases, see https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/releases
]]>
</CHANGES>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
<![CDATA[
old_manifest="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus.plg"
old_runtime="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus"
old_config="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus"
new_config="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2"
marker="$new_config/.migration-v1"

if [[ ! -e "$marker" ]]; then
  mkdir -p "$new_config"

  if [[ -d "$old_config" ]]; then
    for old_cfg in "$old_config"/fanctrlplus_*.cfg; do
      [[ -f "$old_cfg" ]] || continue
      old_name="${old_cfg##*/}"
      new_cfg="$new_config/fanctrlplus2_${old_name#fanctrlplus_}"
      [[ -e "$new_cfg" ]] || cp -p "$old_cfg" "$new_cfg"
    done

    if [[ -f "$old_config/order.cfg" && ! -e "$new_config/order.cfg" ]]; then
      sed 's/fanctrlplus_/fanctrlplus2_/g' "$old_config/order.cfg" > "$new_config/order.cfg.tmp" &&
        mv "$new_config/order.cfg.tmp" "$new_config/order.cfg"
    fi

    if [[ -f "$old_config/pwm_labels.cfg" && ! -e "$new_config/pwm_labels.cfg" ]]; then
      cp -p "$old_config/pwm_labels.cfg" "$new_config/pwm_labels.cfg"
    fi
  fi

  touch "$marker"
fi

if [[ -f "$old_manifest" || -d "$old_runtime" ]]; then
  echo ""
  echo "FanCtrl Plus configuration imported into FanCtrl Plus 2."
  echo "Uninstall upstream FanCtrl Plus, then install FanCtrl Plus 2 again."
  echo "Installation stopped to prevent both plugins controlling the same fans."
  echo ""
  exit 1
fi

exit 0
]]>
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/fanctrlplus2-1.9.4-noarch-1.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new --reinstall">
<URL>https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/releases/download/1.9.4/fanctrlplus2-1.9.4-noarch-1.txz</URL>
<SHA256>b2255c7bf78cd4f88e69d6d71179833485216472172b4aedc179f5b9df9f967b</SHA256>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
<![CDATA[
for package in /boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/fanctrlplus2-*.txz; do
  [[ "$package" == "/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/fanctrlplus2-1.9.4-noarch-1.txz" ]] || rm -f "$package"
done

src="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/rc.fanctrlplus2"
dst="/etc/rc.d/rc.fanctrlplus2"
if [[ -f "$src" ]]; then
  ln -sf "$src" "$dst"
fi

mkdir -p /var/tmp/fanctrlplus2
rm -f /var/tmp/fanctrlplus2/*
pkill -f "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/array_monitor.sh"

if [[ -x "$dst" ]]; then
  "$dst" restart
fi

script="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/array_monitor.sh"
if [[ -x "$script" ]]; then
  nohup "$script" >/dev/null 2>&1 &
fi

echo ""
echo "------------------------------------------------"
echo " FanCtrl Plus 2 is installed."
echo " Version: 1.9.4"
echo "------------------------------------------------"
echo ""
exit 0
]]>
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
<![CDATA[
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/rc.fanctrlplus2 stop 2>/dev/null
pkill -f "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/array_monitor.sh"
removepkg fanctrlplus2 2>/dev/null || true

rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2
rm -rf /boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2
rm -f /etc/rc.d/rc.fanctrlplus2

echo "============================================================"
echo "    FanCtrl Plus 2 has been removed."
echo "    Reboot to restore fan control to BIOS or other tools."
echo "============================================================"
exit 0
]]>
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
